WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.220
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by the CuriosityStream &amp; Nebula bundle,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:10.349
now just 11.59 for an entire year until December
24th.

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:18.750
“I don’t think it’s very useful to speculate
on what god might or might not be able to

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:28.780
do, rather we should examine what he actually
does with the universe we live in, all our

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:36.609
observations suggest that it operates according
to well defined laws.

00:00:36.609 --> 00:00:42.570
These laws may have been ordained by god,
but it seems he does not intervene in the

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:48.429
universe to break the laws, at least once
he set the universe going”

00:00:48.429 --> 00:00:54.769
In the cauldron of the early universe no light
could escape the dense opach fog of primordial

00:00:54.769 --> 00:01:02.280
gas, but as this cosmic soup of atomic particles
began to cool down hydrogen atoms began to

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:09.290
form, leading to the universe's first bright,
violent new starts burning through the fog

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:14.570
that once blocked all light from escaping
the expanding universe.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:20.110
Some of these early photons have travelled
unhindered through the vast empty expanse

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:28.000
of space for 13.5 billion years, and will
reach their destination, here on the man-made

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.450
detectors of the James Webb telescope.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:36.500
A space odyssey coming to an end because of
the curiosity of humans.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:41.700
The James Webb telescope is going to give
us our first detailed glimpse of this early

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.050
universe from which we and everything we know
was born.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:47.050
[1]

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:50.820
The James Webb telescope is a 10 billion dollar
endeavor.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:56.990
An endeavor that has eaten into NASA’s limited
budget, consuming one quarter of NASA’s

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:03.320
entire astronomy budget for years, and in
the early hours of a tenuous launch date of

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:10.000
December 24th, this 10 billion dollar gamble
will launch aboard the Ariane 5 rocket, a

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.719
European heavy lift launch vehicle, from the
European SpacePort in Kourou, French Guiana

00:02:15.719 --> 00:02:22.019
[2] Astronomers, physicists, and enthusiasts
alike will look on with nervous excitement

00:02:22.019 --> 00:02:26.670
as this rocket carries the next generation
in human curiosity.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:37.379
This is the insane engineering of the James
Webb Telescope.

00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:42.549
The combination of technologies required to
make the James Webb telescope possible are

00:02:42.549 --> 00:02:46.430
unique to this time period in human history.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:50.999
The launch vehicle, the image processing,
the electromechanical systems, the cooling

00:02:50.999 --> 00:02:54.239
systems, the mirror, and the sun shield.

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:59.010
This endeavor is the culmination of not just
the decades of work from the engineers and

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:04.650
scientists at NASA, but thousands of years
of work of our ancestors.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:12.599
The materials and engineering required to
peer back 13.5 billion years into the reionization

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:19.769
epoch are a punctuation point in human history,
that we, the human race, should be celebrating

00:03:19.769 --> 00:03:22.959
and watching with bated breath together.

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:28.219
The launch will take place here, in French
Guiana, a space port ideally located on the

00:03:28.219 --> 00:03:34.580
Earth’s equator to give the James Webb Telescope
an extra push towards its final destination.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:40.939
The James Webb Telescope will not be in orbit
around Earth like Hubble, it will be launching

00:03:40.939 --> 00:03:46.610
to a destination 1.5 million kilometres from
Earth, Lagrange point 2.[3]

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:52.010
Lagrange points are special points in space
where small objects, like satellites, can

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:57.309
stay more or less in the same position relative
to the gravitational bodies that they are

00:03:57.309 --> 00:03:59.010
traveling with.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:05.219
This happens because the gravitational pull
from two bodies precisely equals the centripetal

00:04:05.219 --> 00:04:10.129
force required for the object to move with
the gravitational bodies.

00:04:10.129 --> 00:04:16.229
Like little parking spots in space that allow
satellites to sit in a relatively stable position

00:04:16.229 --> 00:04:19.850
while using a minimal amount of fuel to stay
there.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:23.050
There are 5 lagrange points between the Sun
and Earth.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.669
L1 lies between the Sun and Earth.

00:04:25.669 --> 00:04:29.450
It’s extremely useful for Sun observation
satellites.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:35.580
However, the nature of the James Webb telescope's
job wants it to avoid the light from the Sun

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.090
as much as possible.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:44.460
It is an infrared telescope, infrared is heat,
and the heat emanating from the Sun would

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:50.750
completely saturate it’s sensors and make
observing the distant past impossible.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:56.479
So, it will be launching to L2, located about
here.

00:04:56.479 --> 00:05:01.770
Here the telescope can turn it’s back to
the Sun, Earth and Moon, which will stay in

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:08.110
the same position, nicely lined up behind
the telescope thanks to Lagrange point 2’s

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:09.759
unique physics.

00:05:09.759 --> 00:05:15.460
In order to operate correctly the dark side
of the telescope needs to operate at minus

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:18.860
233 degrees celsius (-388 fahrenheit).

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:23.590
Without a way to block out the heat from the
Sun and Earth the telescope would be scorched

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:27.970
at 83 degrees celsius, nearly hot enough to
boil water.

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:28.970
[3]

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:34.180
This is a huge amount of heat to block and
to do this the James Webb Telescope will carry

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:37.550
a massive shield on it’s back, like a tortoise.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:42.889
“And making such a device, is a very very
tough problem.”

00:05:42.889 --> 00:05:48.900
That’s Mike Menzel, Missions Systems Engineer
for the James Webb Telescope.

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:55.590
“We had to map every heat flow to make sure
that we do not let any leak through from the

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:58.650
hot side to the cold side.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:05.259
to make sure that that sunlight, which is
dumping approximately 200,000 watts of power

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:14.090
in our direction - we only want less than
a watt of that to get through to the telescope

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:18.740
and passively cools the telescope.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:19.740
“

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:23.460
Preventing that heat transfer is, as Mike
said, a very tough problem.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:26.540
Heat can transfer in 3 ways.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:32.470
Conduction where heat is transferred from
atom to atom in direct contact with each other,

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:35.110
like heat travelling down a copper pipe.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:40.930
Convection, where heat is transferred from
the physical movement of atoms, and radiation

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:45.140
heat is transferred by electromagnetic waves.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:49.300
In the vacuum of space convection isn’t
a concern.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:55.211
So that leaves conduction and radiation as
methods for heat transfer, let's see how the

00:06:55.211 --> 00:06:58.520
James Webb Telescope is managing these.

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:00.790
First, material choice.

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:07.710
The sunshield needs to be light, strong, resistant
to degradation from solar radiation ,dimensionally

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.939
stable across a range of temperatures, and
reflective.[4]

00:07:10.939 --> 00:07:17.690
That’s a long shopping list of requirements,
and Kapton, a type of high performance plastic,

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:19.980
manages to check all the boxes.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:23.879
Each layer of the kapton sunshield is incredibly
thin.

00:07:23.879 --> 00:07:30.439
Layer 1, the layer closest to the sun is the
thickest at just 0.05 millimeters, while the

00:07:30.439 --> 00:07:34.780
next 4 layers are just 0.025 millimeters thick.

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:35.780
[5]

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:41.120
Kapton by itself is actually mostly transparent,
which isn’t a fantastic trait for a sun

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.760
blocking heat shield.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:49.340
Thankfully, the wonder material that is kapton
can also be easily coated in other materials.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:55.050
Each layer is coated in a 100 nanometer thick
coating of aluminium, giving the sunshield

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:57.219
its reflective appearance.

00:07:57.219 --> 00:08:02.979
This reflective quality helps prevent heat
transfer through radiation, by simply bouncing

00:08:02.979 --> 00:08:09.289
that radiation back to space, and with gaps
between each layer, the heat that is absorbed

00:08:09.289 --> 00:08:15.060
can’t easily transfer through conduction
or convection, taking advantage of the highly

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:19.220
insulating vacuum of space between each layer.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:23.560
Heat could still transfer between each layer
through radiation.

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:29.289
The outermost layer will gain heat and start
glowing with infrared radiation, just as we

00:08:29.289 --> 00:08:32.190
see through an infrared camera.

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:37.310
In order to prevent this the sunshield has
some clever engineering designs.

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:43.370
The layers are angled relative to each other
to ensure the reflected radiation between

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:46.690
each layer is funneled outwards to space.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:52.490
Ensuring that each layer gradually reduces
the temperature as it gets closer to the critical

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:54.740
components in the instrument bay.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:55.740
[6]

00:08:55.740 --> 00:09:01.820
The layers gradually get smaller in area from
layer 1 through 5, ensuring the mirror only

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:07.030
has a direct line of sight with the coldest
layer at all times.

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:13.541
Layer 1 itself is also coated in a special
silicon coating 50 nanometers thick, giving

00:09:13.541 --> 00:09:15.380
it this pink appearance.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:16.380
[7]

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:19.670
Silicon was used because it has high emissivity.

00:09:19.670 --> 00:09:25.240
Simply meaning it emits a lot of the energy
it absorbs out as thermal radiation.

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:30.260
Meaning, the material will not hold onto its
heat, which would give it time to conduct

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:34.850
through the structure of the spacecraft to
areas we want cool.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:41.560
This high emissivity silicon coating is applied
to layer 1 and 2, the two hottest layers,

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:48.160
helping them send their heat back out to space,
away from the spacecraft, as fast as possible.

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:53.390
These design choices are what allow the heat
shield to maintain the massive heat differential

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:59.070
between the hot and cold side, but blocking
heat is just one challenge.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:06.269
“That’s one of the bigger challenges,
along with just designing a deployment system

00:10:06.269 --> 00:10:13.260
that does this complicated and necessary unfolding-
reliably and correctly.

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:14.260
“

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:19.490
In order to fit into the fairing of the Ariane
5 rocket, the sunshield has to be folded and

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:26.310
stowed before launch, leading to some incredibly
complicated mechanics to ensure it unfolds

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:29.330
correctly when gametime arrives.

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:32.440
“deploying things in space is always difficult.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:38.650
But when you’re deploying a rigid structure
- that’s generally what engineers call deterministic…

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:44.810
that’s relatively easy….Membranes and
cables are almost inherently non-deterministic.

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:50.510
And if you want to have or illustrate what
that means - try pushing on a string.

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:51.970
The string will move.

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:57.980
If i ask you to determine the shape that it
will assume you will have a very very hard

00:10:57.980 --> 00:10:59.779
time doing it.

00:10:59.779 --> 00:11:06.540
So to control these almost non-deterministic
things takes a great deal of effort, takes

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.360
a great deal of trial and error.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:14.780
And even after we’re done getting the design
right, the one thing about the sunshield is

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:19.060
it’s almost like a parachute or similar
to a parachute.

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:24.399
You know the parachute will work, but it’s
also only as good as the very very last time

00:11:24.399 --> 00:11:25.589
you fold it.

00:11:25.589 --> 00:11:31.970
And you’re going to find out whether you
folded it correctly or not when you use it.

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:32.970
“

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:37.010
The unfolding process will begin a few days
after launch, not too far from Earth.

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:42.430
[8]Starting with relatively simple mechanisms
with the solar panels and communications antenna

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:43.649
deploying.

00:11:43.649 --> 00:11:49.290
The truly nerve wrecking process begins on
Day 7, as the satellite is coasting towards

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:50.529
L2.

00:11:50.529 --> 00:11:55.600
There are over 300 single points of failure
in this unfolding sequence.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:12:00.890
300 chances for a 10 billion dollar, 25 year
project to end.

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:07.009
107 pins, [9] holding the sunshield together,
have to be released on queue, to allow the

00:12:07.009 --> 00:12:13.540
system of pulleys, motors, cables, bearings
and springs to begin unfurling the sunshield

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:16.260
into its precise complete shape.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:22.399
This process will take 3 days, and once complete
the optical components will unfold and lock

00:12:22.399 --> 00:12:23.990
into place.

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:29.570
Completing the transformation process, but
we are most certainly not in the clear.

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:35.339
The likelihood of the tennis court sized sunshield
being struck by micrometeorites is fairly

00:12:35.339 --> 00:12:41.449
high, and because this a thin layer of plastic
stretched out under tension, a small tear

00:12:41.449 --> 00:12:47.300
caused by an impact could cause a runaway
tear ripping through the whole sunshield.

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:52.779
To prevent this, rip stop seams have been
molded into the sunshield, which will arrest

00:12:52.779 --> 00:12:59.030
tears and keep them confined to a single portion
of the shield without compromising structural

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:00.100
integrity.

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:05.300
The film has also been carefully moulded with
corrugations and other shapes to stiffen and

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:07.320
shape the shield as needed.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:08.320
[10]

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:12.779
This passive cooling system helps tremendously,
ensuring the dark side of the telescope is

00:13:12.779 --> 00:13:19.050
shielded from the sun's heat keeping it’s
sensitive heat detecting instruments at 40

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:23.430
degree Kelvin, about -233 degrees celsius.

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:29.800
But parts of the telescope, specifically the
mid-infrared detection instrument, located

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:33.660
here, needs to be even colder to work correctly.

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:41.899
It needs to be 7 degrees Kelvin, just 7 degrees
off the absolute minimum temperature of the

00:13:41.899 --> 00:13:46.769
universe of zero degrees Kelvin, and for this
we need active cooling.

00:13:46.769 --> 00:13:47.769
[11]

00:13:47.769 --> 00:13:52.930
The James Webb telescope includes an innovative
cryocooler for this purpose.

00:13:52.930 --> 00:14:00.200
The challenge in developing this cryocooler
alone was immense, costing 150 million dollars.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:04.910
Getting cold temperatures is just one small
part of the design.

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:10.199
Vibration has to be eliminated, as the tiniest
movement at the telescope would cause massive

00:14:10.199 --> 00:14:17.170
blurs in the image as it attempts to focus
on objects billions of lightyears away.

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:23.140
That means eliminating moving parts where
possible, and when that can’t be done incredibly

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:28.720
precise machining and movement is needed to
balance weights as they move.

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:33.570
The cooler also needs to use a tiny amount
of electricity, as the telescope only has

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:41.230
2000 watts of power provided by it’s solar
array, and it needs to run reliably for years.

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:46.610
That means a closed loop cycle, with our refrigerant
being continually reused.

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:51.759
I found this explanation of the cryocooler
on NASA’s site.

00:14:51.759 --> 00:14:58.860
The precooler features a two-cylinder horizontally-opposed
pump and cools helium gas using pulse tubes,

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:02.279
which exchange heat with a regenerator acoustically.

00:15:02.279 --> 00:15:08.410
Okay, horizontally opposed pumps, with carefully
balanced pistons that will cut vibrations

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:13.040
as the weights balance each other out, but
the rest of that explanation sounds like it

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:15.420
came straight out of a sci-fi novel.

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:21.350
A sound wave is just a pressure wave and pressure
and temperature are directly proportional.

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:24.030
Higher pressure will cause higher temperature.

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:28.810
One way we can take advantage of this is by
creating a standing wave, where the peaks

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:31.500
and troughs of the wave are stationary.

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:36.819
We can do this in a closed tube where the
resonant frequency of the tube is determined

00:15:36.819 --> 00:15:38.350
by the tube's length.

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:42.910
Here the sound wave will bounce off the closed
end and create a region of compression and

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:46.329
high pressure, and therefore high temperature.

00:15:46.329 --> 00:15:48.750
This alone isn’t terribly useful.

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:54.360
The energy and temperature in this system
will stay relatively stable, left on its own,

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:58.520
but what if we could extract some of this
heat with each cycle?

00:15:58.520 --> 00:16:03.290
Then, on each cycle, we could gradually cool
the overall system.

00:16:03.290 --> 00:16:08.730
To do this, we need a way to pass energy out
of the system, This is done with a stack,

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:13.519
a porous material with air gaps that allow
sound to pass through it, which is placed

00:16:13.519 --> 00:16:18.199
so that it smoothly spans both the hot region
at the end of the tube and the cold region

00:16:18.199 --> 00:16:19.600
in the centre, like this.

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:20.600
[12]

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:25.420
A heat exchanger is then placed on either
end of the stack, one for the hot side and

00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:26.660
one for the cold.

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:31.220
The hot heat exchange will conduct its heat
to the centre of the sunshield, where it can

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:37.130
radiate out to space, while the cold portion
will conduct its heat, or lack thereof, to

00:16:37.130 --> 00:16:42.700
a copper plate attached to the back of the
infrared sensors to cool them to 6.2 degrees

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:43.700
kelvin.

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:44.700
[13]

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:52.430
This is an extreme over simplification of
the actual operation of the pulse tube cryocooler.

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:58.860
This is just a basic explanation of the physical
phenomenon that allows it to work.

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:03.740
The pulse tube cryocooler is quite possibly
the most fascinating part of this spacecraft

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:09.630
to me, utilizing a simple physical phenomenon
with extreme precision.

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:14.850
Allowing those infrared sensors, located in
the centre of the telescope's beautiful golden

00:17:14.850 --> 00:17:16.520
mirror to work.

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:20.209
The golden mirrors are the most striking part
of the telescope.

00:17:20.209 --> 00:17:27.860
Made of 18 hexagonal segments 6.5 metres in
diameter.[14] So, what’s the deal with design?

00:17:27.860 --> 00:17:31.500
It’s unlike any telescope mirror I have
ever seen.

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:35.500
The mirror surface itself is beryllium plated
in gold.

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:39.830
That’s a unique and expensive material choice.

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:46.250
We need the structure of these mirrors to
remain in an extremely precise shape to reflect

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:47.620
light as desired.

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:52.720
They can’t bend and they can’t warp with
temperature changes, and they also need to

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:57.030
be extremely lightweight to reduce launch
costs.

00:17:57.030 --> 00:18:02.010
Beryllium is a lightweight metal, with an
atomic weight of just 4 it’s much lighter

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:07.490
than silica glass, a more traditional mirror
subsurface material, while being far more

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:11.789
capable in dealing with the cryogenic temperatures
the mirror will operate in.

00:18:11.789 --> 00:18:17.250
Keeping its shape and not contracting so much
that it ruins the carefully shaped curves

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:18.600
of the mirror.

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:24.810
While nowhere near as strong as steel, beryllium
is much stiffer with a young’s modulus of

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:26.950
300 Gigapascals [15].

00:18:26.950 --> 00:18:32.500
This means, while the beryllium is easier
to break than steel, it’s harder to deform

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:35.030
before it actually breaks.

00:18:35.030 --> 00:18:37.840
Giving it excellent dimensional stability.

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:42.919
On a pound for pound basis, beryllium is 6
times stiffer than steel.

00:18:42.919 --> 00:18:47.130
[16] Making it the perfect subsurface material
for a mirror.

00:18:47.130 --> 00:18:53.220
However, it is not reflective, and for that
we turn to gold.

00:18:53.220 --> 00:18:58.670
Gold is not the best reflector of visible
light, being particularly poor reflecting

00:18:58.670 --> 00:19:05.280
the lower frequencies of the visible spectrum,
giving it its distinctive golden hue, but

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:12.520
critically it is an excellent reflector of
the infrared spectrum, while being very unreactive,

00:19:12.520 --> 00:19:17.240
ensuring the mirror surface will not tarnish
and lose its shine during its operation.

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:21.000
[17]
To reflect that light a very thin coat, just

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:27.280
0.1 micron in thickness, is coated over the
polished beryllium subsurface.

00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:32.510
Taking just 48.2 grams of gold, about the
same weight as a gold ball.

00:19:32.510 --> 00:19:39.790
A surprisingly small amount for the huge mirror,
which has a collecting area of about 25 m2,

00:19:39.790 --> 00:19:45.360
5.5 times larger than Hubble’s 4.5 metre
squared circular glass mirror.

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:46.360
[18]

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:49.710
The mirror needs to be massive, and to explain
why, I asked Mike Menzel.

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:50.950
So, Mike, why is this mirror so big?

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:58.110
“Well I can tell you it collecting, first
we’re looking for uh, stars or stellar objects

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:01.930
that are approximately going to be a nanojansky.

00:20:01.930 --> 00:20:07.390
And to explain what a nanojansky is, its units
of brightness, very very dim.

00:20:07.390 --> 00:20:08.390
“

00:20:08.390 --> 00:20:11.040
Okay gotcha, could you put that in practical
terms?

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:16.789
“If I were to put a child’s night light,
that’s about 5 watts, put it on the surface

00:20:16.789 --> 00:20:23.310
of the moon and look at it from the Earth,
that source would appear to be 20 nanojanskies.

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:30.110
So we;re looking for objects that are 1/20th
as bright as that.

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:32.350
To do that, you need a big telescope.

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:38.010
Picture light as rain coming in, if you want
to collect a lot of rain you take a big wide

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:39.010
bucket.

00:20:39.010 --> 00:20:46.011
Well even at the size of our bucket, 6 meters
across, we’re only collecting about 1 photon

00:20:46.011 --> 00:20:47.011
per second.

00:20:47.011 --> 00:20:48.250
1 particle of light per second.

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:53.280
And to put that into perspective, i;ll go
out tonight or any night and look at the brightest

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:54.750
star there is in the sky.

00:20:54.750 --> 00:21:02.530
Your eye is probably collecting 1 million
photons per second from that star.

00:21:02.530 --> 00:21:08.020
So to see these very dim things, the dimmest
things there are to see in the universe you

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:12.789
need a light bucket that’s at least 6 meters
in diameter.

00:21:12.789 --> 00:21:13.789
“

00:21:13.789 --> 00:21:17.980
1 photon per second really puts things into
perspective.

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:22.990
Mike and the rest of the team working on the
James Webb Telescope actually wanted the mirror

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:28.000
to be bigger, but the cost of launching a
mirror that size, between the increase in

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:34.700
weight and limited space available inside
the Ariane 5 fairing, was not cost effective.

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:40.810
They maximized the size with the resources
available, and incredibly, even though the

00:21:40.810 --> 00:21:48.260
mirrors collecting surface is 5.5 times larger
than hubbles, the James Webb mirror is 62%

00:21:48.260 --> 00:21:52.039
lighter than Hubble’s massive solid glass
mirror.

00:21:52.039 --> 00:21:58.299
That is an astounding weight saving, driven
by launch weight requirements to get the telescope

00:21:58.299 --> 00:21:59.970
to L2.

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:03.510
And the mirror is even programmable.

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:08.730
When Hubble first began transmitting images
back to earth it became clear that there was

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:12.200
something wrong with the telescope's optics.

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:18.070
Instead of the crisp awe inspiring we are
familiar with today, the early images came

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:26.220
back blurred.[19] The mirror had been ground
down too flat, by a mere 2000 nanometers 1/50th

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:32.890
the thickness of a human hair, but that was
enough to cause the light to be focused incorrectly

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:35.090
on the telescope's sensors.

00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:40.390
Replacing the mirror was not an option, but
Hubble was designed to be serviced throughout

00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:45.929
its lifetime, featuring modular equipment
bays that allowed older equipment to be removed

00:22:45.929 --> 00:22:47.159
and replaced.

00:22:47.159 --> 00:22:52.559
In order to correct the issue, corrective
optics were installed into one of these equipment

00:22:52.559 --> 00:22:59.020
bays, like a giant pair of glasses for the
1.5 billion dollar telescope.

00:22:59.020 --> 00:23:01.210
James Webb will not be serviceable.

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:07.200
It’s simply too far away from earth, beyond
the range of any space vehicles capable of

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:09.890
carrying humans to service it.

00:23:09.890 --> 00:23:14.990
If there was a problem with the mirrors, that
would be game over, but the engineers were

00:23:14.990 --> 00:23:20.900
not taking any chances this time, and have
engineered a system capable of adjusting it’s

00:23:20.900 --> 00:23:23.010
focus by itself.

00:23:23.010 --> 00:23:28.820
Each of the 18 separate mirrors can contort
its shape and adjust its position relative

00:23:28.820 --> 00:23:33.830
to the secondary mirror located in the main
mirror's focal point.

00:23:33.830 --> 00:23:39.251
The weight saving isogrid rear side of the
beryllium mirrors are assembled with a system

00:23:39.251 --> 00:23:45.470
of back plates, struts and motors that can
not only adjust the mirrors rotation, but

00:23:45.470 --> 00:23:50.919
with the centre motor and these struts, the
mirrors can actually change their curvature

00:23:50.919 --> 00:23:56.090
to adjust the focal point of the mirrors,
a feature that could have corrected Hubble's

00:23:56.090 --> 00:23:57.900
issues from Earth.

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:03.910
Once fully deployed the telescope will begin
it’s calibration phase, with each mirror

00:24:03.910 --> 00:24:09.200
adjusting itself until each of the 18 segments
have aligned correctly with the secondary

00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:17.160
mirror, a 0.74 metre convex mirror, which
itself has 6 motors to adjust its position.

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:23.260
These motors and control systems are so precise
that the mirrors can adjust their position

00:24:23.260 --> 00:24:29.890
in steps on the scale of wavelengths of light,
creeping closer to alignment by increments

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:34.390
1/10000th the size of a human hair.

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:39.220
That is an astoundingly accurate electromechanical
system.

00:24:39.220 --> 00:24:44.549
The engineers of the James Webb telescope
performed this calibration test here on earth

00:24:44.549 --> 00:24:50.510
with an absolutely massive vacuum chamber
that can be cooled to the same temperature

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:56.380
that the telescope will operate at, ensuring
proper focus can be achieved.

00:24:56.380 --> 00:25:03.409
But the job to get a clear image isn’t done
with primary and secondary mirror alignment.

00:25:03.409 --> 00:25:10.680
They focus the light onto the cassegrain focus,
which is located inside the aft optics subsystem.

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:15.390
This black protrusion in the middle of the
primary mirror, which blocks stray light from

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:16.550
entering the aperture.

00:25:16.550 --> 00:25:23.429
[20] In the darkness there are two more mirrors,
one of them being the fine steering mirror,

00:25:23.429 --> 00:25:28.870
and this thing is the world's most expensive
image stabilisation tool.

00:25:28.870 --> 00:25:31.809
It is controlled by the fine guiding system.

00:25:31.809 --> 00:25:37.520
The fine guiding system is locked onto a guide
star and it’s job is to keep that star in

00:25:37.520 --> 00:25:40.070
the centre of its field of view.

00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:46.020
Every 64 milliseconds [21] the fine guiding
system will send signals to the attitude control

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:50.870
system to make adjustments to ensure the telescope
stays on target.

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:57.240
This attitude control is done with a combination
of 6 reaction wheels, located inside the spacecraft

00:25:57.240 --> 00:26:02.410
bus, below the heat shield, and with the fine
steering mirror.

00:26:02.410 --> 00:26:07.690
This mirror will constantly be adjusting itself
to ensure the target of the telescope stays

00:26:07.690 --> 00:26:11.380
steady on the sensors, minimizing blur.

00:26:11.380 --> 00:26:14.929
The telescope also has thrusters for larger
position maintenance.

00:26:14.929 --> 00:26:21.659
[22] 191 litre (42 gallons) of hydrazine and
95.5 litres (21 gallons) of it’s oxidizer

00:26:21.659 --> 00:26:28.419
dinitrogen tetroxide will be stored Inside
the spacecraft bus that will feed 20 different

00:26:28.419 --> 00:26:31.870
rocket thrusters scattered around the telescope.

00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:37.640
There are 8 thruster modules, 2 on each corner
of the spacecraft bus, to aid the reaction

00:26:37.640 --> 00:26:42.110
wheels in spinning the telescope to point
towards points of interest.

00:26:42.110 --> 00:26:47.520
These 16 engines will be fed with hydrazine
only, a monopropellant reaction where the

00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:53.290
hydrazine is passed over a catalyst, causing
a highly exothermic reaction, breaking the

00:26:53.290 --> 00:26:57.770
hydrazine down into nitrogen, hydrogen and
ammonia.

00:26:57.770 --> 00:27:03.059
The other 4 motors are for orbital and positional
control, and require more power.

00:27:03.059 --> 00:27:08.330
They will be fed with both hydrazine and dinitrogen
tetroxide.

00:27:08.330 --> 00:27:14.700
This fuel and oxidizer mixture react hypergolically
to form nitrogen and water.

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:20.690
Hypergolic meaning they do not need an igniter,
they simply ignite on contact with each other.

00:27:20.690 --> 00:27:24.730
Hydrazine is an excellent choice for a long
lasting mission like this.

00:27:24.730 --> 00:27:29.910
The hypergolic reaction means the motors can
repeatedly and reliably fire without a point

00:27:29.910 --> 00:27:33.750
of failure causing issues, like an ignitor
breaking.

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:37.480
Hydrazine is also stable for long periods
at room temperature.

00:27:37.480 --> 00:27:43.419
Allowing it to be stored over the expected
10 year life cycle of the James Webb telescope.

00:27:43.419 --> 00:27:49.360
Unfortunately that life cycle is limited to
10 years precisely because of the fuel.

00:27:49.360 --> 00:27:54.110
Between pointing and orbital maintenance,
we will run out of fuel at the same point

00:27:54.110 --> 00:28:00.919
and we currently has no way of refueling the
telescope, but rumors are, behind the scenes,

00:28:00.919 --> 00:28:06.300
NASA is looking to develop the technologies
required to refuel the James Webb telescope

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:09.760
before it’s demise 10 years from now.

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:15.610
Robots capable of refueling spacecraft far
from earth is an exciting concept.

00:28:15.610 --> 00:28:20.789
The James Webb Telescope could end up teaching
us many more fascinating things, beyond the

00:28:20.789 --> 00:28:23.460
early stages of the universe.

00:28:23.460 --> 00:28:30.190
It's my hope as an engineer, after being 25
years on this job, that eventually telescopes,

00:28:30.190 --> 00:28:37.640
the really really big ones of the future,
will be built in space.

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:43.990
Testing James Webb - a telescope that’s
designed to work in space, has been a very

00:28:43.990 --> 00:28:48.120
difficult thing to do on the ground.

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:53.720
And I'm hoping that someday we’ll be building
these things in space, testing them in space,

00:28:53.720 --> 00:28:58.580
tweaking them in space, and then deploying
them in space.

00:28:58.580 --> 00:29:05.010
We are on the frontier of a new space age,
and the James Webb telescope is a milestone

00:29:05.010 --> 00:29:10.390
on our journey towards being a more capable
space faring society.

00:29:10.390 --> 00:29:15.880
This is just one of many milestones in our
brief time as a species capable of escaping

00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:17.370
our planet's gravity.

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:22.159
From building our incredible global position
network and sending satellites to the far

00:29:22.159 --> 00:29:27.510
reaches of our solar system to visiting the
moon and building reusable rockets.

